1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:07,220
You think you've explored medicine. In reality, you're standing in the parking lot of Disney World bragging about how fun

2
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:12,820
the pavement is. You've taken pictures of the asphalt. You've written dissertations about the parking stripes.

3
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:21,910
You've never gone inside. After a century of pharmacology, most beneficial effects of molecules on diseases remain unexplored.

4
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:30,220
Not because biology is constrained. Because clinical trials are slow, expensive, and run by people who think "urgency" is

5
00:00:30,220 --> 00:00:36,960
a type of perfume. Here's the math. It's painful but brief, like a flu shot administered by someone who hates you.

6
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:46,380
Your Tiny Sandbox. Here is everything humanity considers "medicine" after five thousand years of civilization: Twenty thousand

7
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:52,680
products are approved in the U.S. Sounds impressive until you realize most are just "Tylenol, but blue."

8
00:00:53,960 --> 00:00:57,850
These products contain only one thousand six hundred and fifty compounds.

9
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:06,480
Fewer ingredients than a decent French bakery uses. Seven thousand five hundred compounds have passed Phase one globally.

10
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:13,100
Progress! The F.D.A. generally recognized as safe list, or G.R.A.S.

11
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:19,300
list, contains six hundred and thirty-five substances. These are things you could eat right now without dying.

12
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:27,500
Low bar. Still useful. Total unique compounds humanity knows are safe-ish: nine thousand five hundred compounds.

13
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:32,400
That's it. That's your entire medicine cabinet after five millennia.

14
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:42,380
The Target List (Ways Your Body Breaks). The I.C.D.-ten lists fourteen thousand ways your meat can malfunction.

15
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:46,260
Humanity's most comprehensive to-do list, and you're ignoring it.

16
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:52,620
After consolidation, one thousand trial-relevant diseases are worth actually fixing.

17
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:57,830
The Math You're Ignoring. Take your nine thousand five hundred safe compounds.

18
00:01:58,300 --> 00:02:05,660
Multiply by one thousand diseases. This gives you the "Combinatorial Space," which is fancy talk for "stuff you could test

19
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:12,620
tomorrow if you weren't busy filling out forms." Nine thousand five hundred compounds times one thousand diseases equals

20
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:16,600
nine point five million plausible drug-condition combinations.

21
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:23,980
What You've Actually Tested. One thousand seven hundred and fifty unique approved drug-disease pairings.

22
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:30,780
Your entire medical canon. Thirty percent of drugs gain at least one new indication after initial approval.

23
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:37,420
The rest never get a second date. Let's be generous and say you tested ten times as many things that failed.

24
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:44,900
(You did. You learned nothing from them.) Total tested: thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships.

25
00:02:45,780 --> 00:02:53,740
The Scorecard. Thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships tested divided by nine point five million possibilities equals

26
00:02:53,740 --> 00:03:03,560
zero point three four two percent. where: Humanity has explored about a third of one percent of the theoretically testable

27
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:09,420
drug-disease space. You are the detective who searched one drawer and declared the case cold.

28
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:15,260
It's like reading the first page of *War and Peace* and writing a book report called "It's About a Party."

29
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:22,040
Technically accurate. Cosmically insufficient. Is The Untested Stuff Useful?

30
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:27,040
You might argue, "Maybe the other ninety-nine point seven percent is useless!".

31
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:36,040
Biology would like a word. Undrugged Targets: Mapping three hundred and fifty thousand plus clinical trials showed that

32
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:40,460
only twelve percent of the human interactome has ever been targeted by drugs.

33
00:03:41,260 --> 00:03:49,020
You're ignoring eighty-eight percent of your own biology. It's like having a one-hundred-room mansion and living exclusively

34
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:55,140
in the bathroom. Polypharmacology: Drugs are messy. They hit multiple targets.

35
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:59,680
F.D.A.-approved drugs often bind to things you didn't intend.

36
00:04:00,140 --> 00:04:05,520
This means "side effects" are frequently "cures for something else" that nobody bothered to notice.

37
00:04:06,420 --> 00:04:15,500
Sildenafil was developed for chest pain. It failed. Its "side effect" became Viagra, then got repurposed *again* for pulmonary

38
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:24,000
hypertension. Thalidomide was pulled for causing birth defects; its immunomodulatory "side effects" turned out to treat

39
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:31,340
multiple myeloma. Two drugs. Three accidental cures. Zero systematic searches.

40
00:04:32,060 --> 00:04:38,120
You keep finding cures by tripping over them in hallways. Imagine if you opened a door on purpose.

41
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:43,540
Network Overlap: Diseases cluster on shared biological networks.

42
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:54,040
Arthritis and Alzheimer's share biological pathways. Lithium was prescribed for gout in the eighteen hundreds; decades later,

43
00:04:54,420 --> 00:04:57,980
it turned out to be the gold standard treatment for bipolar disorder.

44
00:04:59,060 --> 00:05:03,460
Nobody connected the dots because nobody was drawing lines between diseases.

45
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:10,100
The drug that treats your grandfather's inflammation might fix your grandmother's neurodegeneration.

46
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:16,140
You won't find out, because testing it would require paperwork that takes longer than Grandma has left.

47
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:23,820
The Universe is Big. You Are Small. So far we only counted molecules humanity *already has*.

48
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:29,030
The stuff in your cupboards. If you look at what's chemically possible, it gets embarrassing.

49
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:37,160
Chemical Space: Estimated ten to the twenty-third power to ten to the sixtieth power drug-like molecules.

50
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:42,240
That's more candidates than there are atoms in your body. Or stars in the galaxy.

51
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:51,700
Or excuses from your regulatory agencies. Virtual Libraries: ZINC twenty-two contains tens of billions of purchasable virtual

52
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:59,880
molecules. You could order them online. You haven't. Synthesized: You have made LESS THAN TEN TO THE NEGATIVE FORTIETH POWER

53
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:07,600
of possible molecules. Tested divided by possible equals approximately ten to the negative twentieth power to ten to the

54
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:13,500
negative fiftieth power. Effectively zero. You haven't even started.

55
00:06:14,120 --> 00:06:22,040
This is like exploring Earth by looking at one grain of sand on one beach in New Jersey, then declaring you've "seen the

56
00:06:22,040 --> 00:06:27,120
world." You haven't. You've seen a grain of sand. In New Jersey.

57
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:35,140
Why You Are Still Sick. It's not because science is hard. It's because you've built a system that is allergic to answers.

58
00:06:35,820 --> 00:06:45,880
Trials Cost Too Much. A typical Phase two to three randomized controlled trial, or R.C.T., costs thirty to one hundred million

59
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:49,980
dollars (Median cost per patient forty-one thousand dollars).

60
00:06:50,820 --> 00:06:56,780
At that price, you only test "sure things." Safe bets. Boring bets.

61
00:06:56,900 --> 00:07:05,100
Bets that don't cure cancer. Clinical trials are a velvet-rope nightclub where the bouncer is a regulatory agency and the

62
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:14,900
cover charge is a hundred million dollars. Herd Mentality. Trials overwhelmingly test the same few biological targets due

63
00:07:14,900 --> 00:07:21,500
to preferential attachment dynamics. Everyone studies what everyone else studies because that's how you get grants.

64
00:07:22,140 --> 00:07:28,380
It's like if every explorer only went to France because France already had good reviews on TripAdvisor.

65
00:07:29,300 --> 00:07:34,300
Meanwhile, undiscovered continents full of cures sit there, undiscovered.

66
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:45,280
No Profit. Most molecules have no patent. No patent equals no one hundred million dollar trial which equals no approval.

67
00:07:45,560 --> 00:07:53,940
Metformin, a diabetes drug from the nineteen fifties, shows persistent signals for reducing cancer risk and extending lifespan.

68
00:07:54,320 --> 00:08:02,980
It costs pennies. It's off-patent. The trial to prove it works for aging (the Targeting Aging with Metformin trial, or T.A.M.E.)

69
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:10,110
spent years begging for funding because no pharmaceutical company will bankroll a drug they can't monopolize.

70
00:08:11,020 --> 00:08:16,580
The molecule that saves your life has to make money first. If it can't, it won't.

71
00:08:17,260 --> 00:08:25,640
You die. Quarterly earnings go up. Neglect. If you have a rare disease with no market, you don't get a drug.

72
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:33,799
Your cells malfunction in an unprofitable way. The invisible hand of the market has weighed your life against quarterly

73
00:08:33,799 --> 00:08:39,679
earnings and found you wanting. Sorry about your death. It was a business decision.

74
00:08:40,940 --> 00:08:48,960
How Long to Explore Everything? At the current pace of three thousand three hundred trials per year, how long would it take

75
00:08:48,960 --> 00:08:56,020
to systematically test therapeutic possibilities? Tier 1: Single Compounds (Most Conservative).

76
00:08:56,920 --> 00:09:05,660
Testing every known safe compound against every major disease: Nine point five million combinations divided by three thousand

77
00:09:05,660 --> 00:09:11,440
three hundred trials per year equals two thousand eight hundred and eighty years.

78
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:19,460
That's almost two thousand eight hundred and eighty years to explore compounds we ALREADY know are safe.

79
00:09:20,200 --> 00:09:23,850
By that math, we won't finish until the year five thousand.

80
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:33,000
Tier 2: Combination Therapies (Still Defensible). Modern medicine increasingly relies on multi-drug cocktails.

81
00:09:34,940 --> 00:09:40,700
Oncology, H.I.V., cardiology, psychiatry: all routinely use drug combinations.

82
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:47,220
So the real question isn't "does this one drug work?" It's "which combinations work?"

83
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:56,760
Testing pairwise combinations of safe compounds: Forty-five point one billion combinations divided by three thousand three

84
00:09:56,760 --> 00:10:03,040
hundred trials per year equals thirteen point seven million years.

85
00:10:03,720 --> 00:10:11,460
That's longer than Homo sapiens has existed as a species. And that's using only compounds with established safety data,

86
00:10:12,220 --> 00:10:17,090
testing only pairs. Not triplets. God help you if you want to try triplets.

87
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:27,520
Tier 3: The Full Chemical Universe (Illustrative). Beyond known compounds lies the chemical space of ten to the power of

88
00:10:27,520 --> 00:10:32,050
twenty-three to ten to the power of sixty drug-like molecules.

89
00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:40,020
At current trial capacity, exploring even the synthesizable fraction would take longer than the universe has existed.

90
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:46,380
The universe is thirteen point eight billion years old. You need more time than that.

91
00:10:46,760 --> 00:10:53,400
To test pills. Pills that already exist. We haven't even started.

92
00:10:54,100 --> 00:11:01,780
"But Won't We Run Out of Easy Discoveries?" Some will argue "diminishing returns": the more we search, the harder each new

93
00:11:01,780 --> 00:11:06,220
discovery becomes. This assumes we've already picked the low-hanging fruit.

94
00:11:06,940 --> 00:11:12,500
We haven't picked *any* fruit. We're still in the parking lot arguing about whether fruit exists.

95
00:11:13,420 --> 00:11:18,640
Diminishing returns only kicks in after you've explored a significant fraction of the space.

96
00:11:19,180 --> 00:11:27,080
At less than one percent explored, every trial teaches you something new about human biology, improving your ability to

97
00:11:27,180 --> 00:11:32,680
predict what works next. Learning compounds. Depletion is negligible.

98
00:11:33,260 --> 00:11:38,160
You are nowhere near the bottom of the barrel because you have not yet located the barrel.

99
00:11:39,040 --> 00:11:47,300
The math: even with weak learning, diminishing returns only matters after exploring approximately thirty-seven percent of

100
00:11:47,420 --> 00:11:55,720
therapeutic space. For combination therapies, that crossover is over five million years away at current pace (thirty-seven

101
00:11:55,720 --> 00:12:02,500
percent of forty-five point one billion combinations at three thousand three hundred trials per year).

102
00:12:03,240 --> 00:12:07,120
You cannot have diminishing returns when you've barely started looking.

103
00:12:07,860 --> 00:12:16,740
See the formal analysis for the equations. The Fix. The Oxford RECOVERY Trial (COVID-nineteen) proved that large-scale pragmatic

104
00:12:16,740 --> 00:12:23,720
trials can run for approximately five hundred dollars and deliver results in less than one hundred days.

105
00:12:24,520 --> 00:12:29,600
Five hundred dollars versus forty-one thousand dollars. Read those numbers again.

106
00:12:30,120 --> 00:12:38,500
One of them is the reason people are dying. With a decentralized F.D.A., trial capacity increases by twelve point three

107
00:12:38,500 --> 00:12:48,040
times: Nine point five million combinations divided by forty thousand seven hundred trials per year equals two hundred and

108
00:12:48,040 --> 00:12:54,360
thirty-four years. Still a long time, but at least it's a number with fewer digits than a phone book.

109
00:12:55,140 --> 00:13:01,840
And the first few decades would capture the highest-value discoveries (the low-hanging fruit that current trial scarcity

110
00:13:01,840 --> 00:13:05,740
forces you to ignore while people die in the waiting room).

111
00:13:06,620 --> 00:13:14,760
More importantly, your decentralized F.D.A. builds *permanent infrastructure* for systematic therapeutic exploration.

112
00:13:15,220 --> 00:13:23,100
The goal isn't to "finish." It's to stop leaving cures on the table while you form subcommittees to discuss whether to form

113
00:13:23,100 --> 00:13:27,960
committees to review the proposal to consider them. The Bottom Line.

114
00:13:28,460 --> 00:13:37,360
Humanity has empirically tested less than zero point three four two percent of the drug-disease relationships that can be

115
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:46,600
tested using existing safe compounds. If you include the larger chemical universe, humanity has explored roughly one-quadrillionth

116
00:13:46,840 --> 00:13:52,320
to one-quintillionth of what is possible (ten to the power of negative twelve to ten to the power of negative fifty).

117
00:13:53,400 --> 00:13:57,580
There are numbers so small that mathematicians get depressed looking at them.

118
00:13:58,300 --> 00:14:05,260
These are those numbers. The overwhelming majority of therapeutic potential remains untouched.

119
00:14:05,820 --> 00:14:11,520
Not because cures are impossible. Because trials are too expensive and too slow.

120
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:20,000
A decentralized, automated pragmatic trial system is the only route to systematically explore the remaining ninety-nine

121
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:27,700
point nine nine nine percent of medical space. You're not in the parking lot because there's nothing inside Disney World.

122
00:14:28,100 --> 00:14:34,000
You're in the parking lot because the entrance fee is one hundred million dollars and requires seventeen years of paperwork.

123
00:14:35,180 --> 00:14:38,990
People are dying in the parking lot. The rides are right there.

124
00:14:39,220 --> 00:14:42,260
You can hear the screaming. (Fun screaming. Cure screaming.).

125
00:14:43,140 --> 00:14:45,940
The cures are inside. Open the gate.
